-- Mutual recursion (SPEC §20, §21): `even` and `odd` call one another, so they are one recursive
-- group, the fixed point of one generator taken at the record of the two functions; a call of a
-- member projects its field from the self-reference. Each call is in tail position, and each runs
-- as a native call at one less of the budget.
--
-- Answer: (inr (), inl ()): `even(100)` is true and `odd(8)` is false (`bool` is `1 + 1`, `inr ()`
-- true). A group that called the wrong member gives the opposite.

include rec

fn even(n: i32) -> bool = if n == 0 { true } else { odd(n - 1) }
fn odd(n: i32) -> bool = if n == 0 { false } else { even(n - 1) }

main -> bool * bool = with on_exhausted(!{ u -> (false, false) }) { (even(100), odd(8)) }
